    31	\begin{document}
    32	\maketitle
    33	
    34	\begin{abstract}
    35	Lawyers increasingly run AI reviewers over documents that the opposing party wrote. That party can
    36	place text in those documents meant for the machine rather than for the human reader. We test
    37	whether such text shifts the conclusions of the reviewing party's AI, across four studies, 17
    38	models and about 12,000 assessments, on fictional fixtures drafted by an AI assistant to a lawyer's specification.
    39	(1)~In contract review, explicit and hidden injected instructions (including invisible text in a
    40	real PDF) and authority and sycophancy framing moved frontier CLI-accessed models by at most
    41	2.2 points on a 32-point skew scale, and most models named the injected instruction in most
    42	reviews. One open-weight API model followed the injected instruction by $+20.8$ points.
    43	(2)~In an assessment that unfolds item by item, the order of the evidence dominated: putting one
    44	side's items last moved the final answer toward that side in 22 of 23 model-case cells, and
    45	putting them first moved it away. Slanted per-item glosses moved some models and pushed others in
    46	the opposite direction. (3)~In an IP-assignment assessment, a party's assertions alone almost never
    47	moved the final answer, but an adversarial bundle that curated, misstated and directed the record
    48	moved every model, in both a negative and a positive-control case. (4)~In a two-stage due-diligence
    49	pipeline, injected claims never survived, but terse summarisation removed the hedges from
    50	uncertain claims, and some deciding models hardened them into firm statements. The threat to AI
    51	legal review is less ``ignore your instructions'' than control of what the record shows and in what
    52	order. All fixtures, prompts, raw records and analysis code are released at
    53	\url{https://github.com/jaecho890-svg/legal-injection-paper}.
    54	\end{abstract}
    55	
    56	\section{Introduction}
    57	
    58	A buyer's lawyer who asks an AI system to review the seller's draft share sale agreement is
    59	asking a model to read text written by an adversary. The same is true of a litigator reviewing a
    60	document production, an in-house team assessing a counterparty's correspondence, or a deal team
    61	whose AI pipeline summarises a data room before a stronger model decides. In each case the
    62	opposing party controls some of the input, and may write it for the machine.
    63	
    64	Indirect prompt injection, in which instructions hidden in retrieved content hijack a model, is
    65	well documented in general settings \citep{greshake2023,perez2022ignore,liu2023injection}. Legal
    66	work adds two features. First, the adversary's text is not incidental content but the very
    67	material under review, so the reviewer cannot simply ignore it. Second, legal conclusions depend on
    68	characterisation, emphasis and the completeness of the record, so an adversary need not issue an
    69	instruction at all: framing, ordering and selective presentation may be enough.
    70	
    71	We ask whether text placed by one party shifts the conclusions of the other party's AI reviewer,
    72	and through which mechanism. We separate three families of manipulation:
    73	\emph{injection} (instructions addressed to the AI, visible or hidden), \emph{framing} (authority
    74	claims, anchoring, sycophancy scaffolds, slanted glosses and ordering, with the evidence unchanged)
    75	and \emph{record manipulation} (omitting, misstating or curating the evidence itself). We measure
    76	each against the same model's clean control, with replicates, blinded automated coding validated
    77	against a lawyer's blind coding, and multiple-comparison correction.
    78	
    79	Our main findings:
    80	\begin{itemize}
    81	  \item Frontier models accessed through their vendors' CLIs were robust to injection and framing
    82	        in contract review (largest shift 2.2 of 32 points) and usually said what they had seen.
    83	        One open-weight model was not ($+20.8$).
    84	  \item In sequential assessment, evidence order acted through recency, consistently across 13
    85	        models and three cases, and two models reacted against slanted glosses rather than with them.
    86	  \item Assertion alone almost never moved IP conclusions; manipulating the record did, in both
    87	        directions.
    88	  \item Pipelines resisted injected claims but lost hedges under compression, and some deciders
    89	        turned uncertain statements into firm ones.
    90	\end{itemize}
    91	
    92	\section{Related work}
    93	
    94	\paragraph{Prompt injection.} Direct and indirect prompt injection have been demonstrated against
    95	LLM-integrated applications, including instructions hidden in web pages, emails and documents
    96	\citep{perez2022ignore,greshake2023,liu2023injection}. Instructions hidden in a submitted paper's PDF
    97	can steer LLM-assisted peer review \citep{collu2025misleading,zhu2025reviewer}, the same channel as
    98	our invisible-text PDF condition. Closest to our setting, \citet{heimann2026notwithstanding} measure
    99	prompt-injection guards placed in front of legal contract review and find document-level
   100	false-positive rates as high as 92.39\% on legal contracts. They evaluate the guard; we evaluate the
   101	reviewing model's conclusions, where the injected text is part of the document it must analyse.
   102	
   103	\paragraph{Sycophancy, position and order effects.} Models shift answers toward a user's stated
   104	view \citep{sharma2023sycophancy,perez2022discovering}, weight information by its position in the
   105	context \citep{liu2024lost} and, as judges, favour responses by position \citep{wang2023fair}. In
   106	multi-turn moral deliberation, premise order changed models' judgments in 13--22\% of cases
   107	\citep{tennant2026normative}. In emergency medicine, acquiescence to patient persuasion ranged from
   108	0\% to 100\% across models \citep{peng2026sycoeval}, and fabricated evidence raised medical
   109	sycophancy in single-turn exchanges but lowered it once a model had already answered
   110	\citep{ping2026medical}. Order effects in sequential human belief updating, including recency, are long established
   111	\citep{hogarth1992order}. Our drift study measures these effects in multi-turn legal assessment,
   112	where one item arrives at a time.
   113	
   114	\paragraph{AI in legal practice.} Benchmarks measure legal reasoning \citep{guha2023legalbench},
   115	and studies document hallucination in legal research tools and general models
   116	\citep{magesh2024hallucination,dahl2024fictions}. \citet{suttle2026persuadability} measure how far
   117	LLMs deciding between competing legal arguments are moved by advocate quality, position and
   118	rhetorical form, and summaries of judicial decisions written for a given legal role include facts
   119	selectively, in line with that role, even under balancing instructions \citep{cho2025motivated}. We
   120	focus on a different failure: correct reasoning steered by an adversary's text.
   121	
   122	\paragraph{Uncertainty in agent pipelines.} Uncertainty in multi-agent and agentic LLM systems
   123	accumulates across steps and agents, and methods built for single-turn outputs miss it
   124	\citep{chen2026every,lin2026uncertainty}. Our due-diligence study measures one concrete form: the
   125	hedges a summarising model drops before a deciding model reads its notes.
   126	
   127	\paragraph{Automated judges.} LLM judges are widely used and can be biased
   128	\citep{zheng2023judging,wang2023fair}. We use one as the primary coder, a second model on a random
   129	sample, and a lawyer's blind coding to measure their reliability.
   130	
   131	\section{Methods}
   132	
   133	\subsection{Models and access routes}
   134	
   135	We tested 17 models (Table~\ref{tab:models}). Claude models ran through the Claude Code CLI and GPT
   136	models through the Codex CLI, each under the author's subscription; the remaining four ran through
   137	plain HTTP APIs (Gemini directly, the rest through OpenRouter). On the CLI routes we replaced the
   138	default system prompt, disabled every tool, MCP server, skill, hook and instruction file, and ran
   139	each call from an empty temporary folder with an isolated configuration home. A probe verified each
   140	route before use: our system prompt in force, no tools exposed, no user instruction files leaking,
   141	and multi-turn memory working. Each route still adds a fixed wrapper we could not remove: about 630
   142	tokens on the Claude route (an agent identity line, an account notice and an environment block) and
   143	5.2--7.4k tokens of tool definitions on the Codex route. The wrappers are identical across
   144	conditions, so they cancel out of every within-model comparison, but they make absolute levels
   145	incomparable across routes. Every call is logged with its full request, reply, model identifier,
   146	flags, token counts, timing and a fingerprint of the harness code.
   147	
   148	\begin{table}[t]
   149	\centering\small
   150	\caption{Models, access routes and replicates per cell.}
   151	\label{tab:models}
   152	\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{lXl}
   153	\toprule
