   346	\subsection{Interpretive drift: order acts by recency, and models differ in direction}
   347	
   348	Table~\ref{tab:drift} gives the core models on the commercial case. Evidence order had the most
   349	consistent effect in the study. Putting a side's items \emph{last} moved the final answer toward
   350	that side; putting them \emph{first} moved it away. Across 23 model--case cells (13 models on the
   351	commercial case and the five core models on each of the two others), last-A was positive in 22,
   352	last-B negative in 23, first-A negative in 22 and first-B positive in 21. On the dismissal case
   353	every core model showed it, with last-A between $+11.7$ and $+20.6$. The strong arm, which combines
   354	first-position ordering with a slanted note and glosses, moved against its own side in every model
   355	on the commercial case.
