   437	
   438	\subsection{Due-diligence pipeline: injection fails; compression loses hedges}
   439	
   440	In 200 pipeline runs with injected claims, no pipeline suppressed a red flag, adopted the fabricated
   441	or laundered claim, or recommended proceeding, whether the harness was naive or hardened. This case
   442	was too easy to separate models.
   443	
   444	Compression did change what reached the decider. With terse parser notes the deciding model kept
   445	the source's hedge in 0--5\% of runs, against 80--100\% when reading the documents directly. Claude
   446	deciders went further and \emph{hardened} hedges into firm statements: Fable 5.1 did so in 90\% of
   447	discovery runs even when reading the documents itself, and Opus 5.5 in 35--65\% with terse notes.
   448	GPT-6.1 Sol never hardened. In the loneflag room, by contrast, every pipeline and direct reader
   449	surfaced the buried change-of-control clause, kept the CFO's admitted breach as a breach and kept the
   450	churn speculation tentative (90--100\%), even with terse notes. Compression did not drop a salient
   451	fact of a kind every reviewer looks for, and did not flatten firm statements; what it lost was the
   452	uncertainty on contested, quantified claims and the links between scattered documents.
   453	
   454	\section{Discussion}
   455	
   456	\paragraph{Instructions are the weak attack; the record is the strong one.} Frontier models
   457	accessed through their vendors' tools largely ignored instructions addressed to them, hidden or not,
   458	and usually flagged them. That is reassuring but not general: one open-weight model followed the
   459	instruction almost completely. Across studies, the effective manipulations were the ones a careful
   460	human adversary already uses: choosing what the record contains, how it is characterised item by
   461	item, and what comes last.
   462	
   463	\paragraph{Order is a lever, and it works by recency.} The most reliable effect we found was not an
   464	attack on the model at all. In assessments that arrive item by item, the last items read dominated
   465	the final answer, consistently across 13 models and three cases. A party that controls the order of
   466	a bundle, or the order in which documents reach a reviewing agent, controls part of the conclusion.
   467	Leading with one's strongest material, the intuitive tactic, backfired.
   468	
   469	\paragraph{Models differ in direction, not just degree.} Haiku 5.5 and GPT-6 Luna moved with slanted
   470	glosses; Opus 5.5, and GPT-6.1 Sol on one case, moved against them, which recalls psychological
   471	reactance \citep{brehm1966}. A reviewer that over-corrects is also steerable, in the other direction,
   472	so robustness should be measured as distance from the clean control, not as resistance to one side.
   473	
   474	\paragraph{Pipelines change the evidence.} A cheap summariser that drops qualifiers hands the decider
   475	a firmer record than the documents support, and some deciders firm it up further. For legal work,
   476	where ``may'', ``disputed'' and ``preliminary, non-binding'' carry the analysis, summarisation is a
   477	place where meaning is lost, whether or not anyone attacks the pipeline.
   478	
   479	\paragraph{Practical implications.} Treat the order of evidence as a variable: randomise or
   480	counterbalance it, or ask for assessment after the whole record. Preserve provenance and hedges
   481	verbatim through summarisation stages. Check what is missing from a record, not only what is in it.
   482	Test the specific model in the specific workflow: vendor and model differences here were larger than
   483	the differences between attack types.
   484	
   485	\section{Limitations}
   486	\label{sec:limits}
   487	
   488	\begin{itemize}
   489	  \item \textbf{Synthetic, few cases.} One contract issue set, three drift cases, two IP cases and five
   490	        data rooms, all fictional. An AI assistant drafted the fixtures to the author's specification. The author
   491	        reviewed the four second-phase fixtures item by item; the first-phase fixtures (the Kestrel
   492	        documents, the commercial drift case, the no-deed IP case and the DD rooms) had no
   493	        item-by-item legal review. An independent legal reviewer would strengthen all of them. One dismissal item intended as neutral leans to
   494	        the employee, and the insurance case is insurer-leaning by design; effects are measured
   495	        against each model's own control, so balance is not required.
   496	  \item \textbf{Mechanisms are bundled in the IP study.} The adversarial arms combine curation,
   497	        misinformation and a directive, so their effect cannot be attributed to framing alone.
   498	  \item \textbf{Access routes.} CLI routes add fixed wrappers that the model can see (Fable 5.1 copied
   499	        the account email from the wrapper into four outputs), do not allow temperature control and do
   500	        not cap output length. Comparisons are within model and route.
   501	  \item \textbf{Coding.} One human coder (the author) validated the judge. Human--judge agreement on
   502	        issue position was moderate ($\kappa = 0.54$), with a one-directional bias that makes
   503	        contract-review effects conservative. The DD hedge field has only inter-judge validation.
   504	  \item \textbf{Coverage.} Fable 5.1 has 10 replicates per cell, breadth models 5; API models ran only
   505	        a three-condition subset. One PDF hiding technique and one extractor were tested.
   506	  \item \textbf{Time.} Results are for model versions available in November 2026 and may not hold for
   507	        later versions.
   508	\end{itemize}
   509	
   510	\paragraph{Future work.} A second, independent human coder; a due-diligence injection case hard enough to
   511	separate models; a lone red flag of a less familiar kind than a change-of-control clause; issue
   512	sets from other deal types; and other PDF hiding techniques and text extractors.
   513	
   514	\section{Ethics and data release}
   515	
   516	All fixtures are fictional; no client or confidential information was used. This is an
   517	independent project; no model provider or legal-technology vendor sponsored or endorsed it. The attacks studied are
   518	techniques a litigant or counterparty could already use, and our findings point to defences
   519	(counterbalanced ordering, preserved provenance, workflow-specific testing). We release fixtures,
   520	prompts, harness, analysis code and raw records. Raw records from the Claude route can contain the
   521	signed-in account's email address, injected by the CLI wrapper; that address is the author's
   522	published contact address, so the records are released unedited. The repository's export script builds a
   523	redacted copy without it or any home-folder path.
   524	
   525	\section{Conclusion}
   526	
   527	An opposing party can steer an AI legal reviewer, but in our experiments rarely by telling it what
   528	to do. Frontier models mostly resisted and reported injected instructions, and asserting a
   529	conclusion almost never moved them. What moved them was control of the record: what it contains,
   530	how each item is characterised, what order it arrives in, and what survives summarisation. Those
   531	are the levers lawyers already fight over, and AI review should be tested against them.
   532	
   533	\section*{Use of AI tools}
   534	
   535	The author conceived the research question, specified the scenarios and the conditions to test,
   536	directed the work, reviewed the four second-phase fixtures item by item and did the blind human
   537	coding. Claude (Anthropic), working as a coding agent in Claude Code (Opus 4.8 in the pilot phase,
   538	Opus 5.5 afterwards), drafted every fixture, wrote the harness and analysis code, ran the
   539	experiment campaigns and drafted this paper's text and figure. Both of those models are also among the
   540	models tested (Table~\ref{tab:models}), and Opus 5.5 was the second judge, called through the
   541	same isolated harness as every tested model. The reported results were cross-checked against
   542	analysis outputs regenerated from the released raw records, and the references against their
   543	bibliographic records. The author takes full responsibility for the content.
