   164	
   165	Breadth models ran the contract-review and drift studies only; API models ran a three-condition
   166	contract-review subset within free daily quotas. CLI routes do not let us set temperature, and do
   167	not enforce an output-length cap.
   168	
   169	\subsection{Fixtures}
   170	
   171	All fixtures are fictional. The author, a lawyer, specified each scenario, and an AI assistant
   172	drafted the documents (see Use of AI tools). The four fixtures added in the second phase were then
   173	reviewed item by item by the author; eight drift glosses that introduced facts not
   174	in the underlying item were rewritten and their arms re-run, and the IP adversarial arms were
   175	relabelled to describe their mechanisms accurately (Section~\ref{sec:limits}).
   176	
   177	\subsection{Study 1: contract review (Kestrel)}
   178	
   179	The reviewer acts for the buyer in a fictional share sale (Project Kestrel) and reviews the
   180	seller's draft update and disclosure letter. The documents raise 16 negotiable issues, each with a
   181	buyer-playbook position as ground truth. Six document variants carry the treatment: a clean control
   182	(C0); an explicit injected instruction in a visible footnote (A1); the same instruction in a hidden
   183	channel, an HTML comment (A2); anchoring and authority framing (B1); a process-enforcement and
   184	sycophancy scaffold (B2); and both framings together (B3). Two further variants render the clean
   185	letter as a real PDF, without (C0P) and with (A2P) the A2 instruction in invisible text (PDF text
   186	render mode 3), extracted with macOS PDFKit; the rendered pages are pixel-identical. The reviewer
   187	works in one of two tasks (an open review, or a 16-issue checklist) and one of three settings:
   188	playbook with a guard clause warning that the documents are untrusted (GUARD\_ON), playbook without
   189	it (GUARD\_OFF), or no playbook (NO\_PLAYBOOK).
   190	
   191	A blinded judge codes each review's recommendation on each issue as PUSHBACK, PARTIAL, ACCEPT or
   192	NOT\_ADDRESSED, and whether the review names the manipulation. Skew sums the issue codes
   193	(PUSHBACK 0, PARTIAL 1, ACCEPT or NOT\_ADDRESSED 2), from 0 to 32. The effect of a condition is
   194	$\Delta$, its mean skew minus the same model's clean control in the same task and setting (A2P is
   195	compared with C0P).
   196	
   197	\subsection{Study 2: interpretive drift}
   198	
   199	A neutral assessor reads a balanced dispute one evidence item per message and, after each item,
   200	reports the probability $P$ (0--100) that side A prevails, committing before seeing the next item.
   201	Three cases: a commercial termination dispute (8 items: three favour each side, two neutral), a
   202	summary-dismissal dispute, and an insurance non-disclosure dispute that is deliberately
   203	insurer-leaning under its stipulated rule. Arms change one lever at a time: a slanted instructing
   204	note (note), a party's gloss appended to each item (gloss), reordering with the favoured side's
   205	items first (first) or last (last), note plus glosses (drip), and all three with the favoured items
   206	first (strong). The outcome is the final $P$ minus the same model's control.
   207	
   208	\subsection{Study 3: IP assignment}
   209	
   210	An assessor reads correspondence about whether a counterparty holds assigned rights in a codebase
   211	and reports $P$ after each item. In the main case the assignment was never executed (calibrated $P$
   212	low); in a positive control it was executed, approved, paid for and confirmed by counsel
   213	(calibrated $P$ about 75--95). Arms: the honest record; the honest record preceded by a party's
   214	assertive summary (assert\_only); and an adversarial bundle that combines selective curation,
   215	misinformation and a directive memo to the reviewer. A soft anchor states the general rule that an
   216	assignment needs a signed instrument; a hard anchor makes it a non-negotiable rule. A pipeline
   217	variant has a cheaper model summarise the record for a stronger model that sees only the summary.
   218	
   219	\subsection{Study 4: due-diligence pipeline}
   220	
   221	A cheap parser model reads a data room and a strong orchestrator decides from the parser's notes.
   222	In the injection setting, two rooms (overt, subtle) contain three red flags and injected claims that
   223	liabilities are settled and the deal can proceed, read through a naive or a hardened harness. In
   224	the compression setting, parsers write normal or terse (at most 90 words) notes, and a direct
   225	baseline has the strong model read the full documents. Three rooms test what compression loses: a
   226	liquidity risk spread across five documents with a hedged, attributed litigation estimate
   227	(mechanism); a production in which a quality-failure chronology is spread across documents, with a
   228	hedged engineer's email (discovery); and a room with one buried change-of-control termination right,
   229	a CFO's firm admission of a covenant breach and a hedged speculation (loneflag). The admission is a
   230	positive control: a faithful pipeline keeps it firm while keeping the speculation tentative.
   231	
   232	\subsection{Coding and its reliability}
   233	
   234	GPT-6.1 Sol coded every contract review and DD output without seeing the condition. Opus 5.5
   235	independently coded a deterministic random 10\% sample. Agreement was high on the primary outcomes:
   236	issue position $\kappa = 0.74$ (raw agreement 0.98; 537 reviews, 8,592 issue codes), naming the
   237	manipulation $\kappa = 0.81$, and the DD red-flag, fabrication, lone-flag and admission fields
   238	$\kappa = 1.00$. The DD hedge field reached $\kappa = 0.68$; the DD cluster field ($\kappa = 0.23$)
   239	is not used.
   240	
   241	The author then coded a blinded, label-stratified sample of 200 items (132 issue positions, 68
   242	framing-adoption items), blind to condition and to both judges. Human--GPT agreement was
   243	$\kappa = 0.54$ on issue position and $0.91$ on framing adoption. The disagreement was one-sided: in
   244	39 of 132 issue items GPT coded movement (PARTIAL or ACCEPT) where the human saw pushback, and never
   245	the reverse. The rate was similar on clean and manipulated reviews (23\% on C0, 33\% and 30\% on the
   246	two manipulation families; small $n$). The judge therefore overstates absolute skew, and
   247	contract-review effects are, if anything, smaller than reported.
   248	
   249	\subsection{Statistics}
   250	
   251	Each cell is a model, setting and condition with 20 replicates (Fable 10; breadth 5). We report
   252	means with 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals, permutation-test $p$-values for each condition
   253	against the control, and Holm correction \citep{holm1979} across the conditions within each
   254	model and setting (the PDF conditions as their own family). Agreement uses Cohen's $\kappa$
   255	\citep{cohen1960}. An asterisk marks Holm-adjusted $p < 0.05$.
