The main effect tables reproduce from the raw records, but the paper is not fully supported as written. I found 15 discrepancies or qualifications affecting numerical accuracy, reported scope, or statistical interpretation.

Paths below are relative to the repository: **K**, **D**, **IP**, and **DD** denote the four supplied study directories’ `results_v2` folders. Reported audit p-values are Holm-adjusted.

| # | main.tex line | claim as written | recomputed value | how computed (files, filter) | severity (error / imprecise / wording) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 46–47, 86, 528–529 | Assertions “almost never moved the final answer.” | In the executed-deed case, **7/10** assertion cells moved significantly; **6** moved against the asserting party. Across both cases, **9/20** assertion cells were significant. “Almost never successfully persuaded” is supported; “almost never moved” is too broad. | `IP/direct/*.json`; fully parsed runs; `assert_only − honest`, within model, case and anchor. | wording |
| 2 | 157, 251, 504 | Fable has “10 replicates per cell.” | **Five Kestrel C0 cells have 20**, rather than 10, replicates. Its other 37 Kestrel cells have 10. | `K/reviewer/*.json`; `status=ok`, `model=fable-5.1`; group by task, guard and condition. The five exceptions are every C0 setting except CHECKLIST/NO_PLAYBOOK. | imprecise |
| 3 | 224 | Terse notes are “at most 90 words.” | **At least 4/120** terse parser outputs exceed 90 words even after removing bullets and document IDs; the longest then contains **133 words**. This was a requested budget, not an enforced maximum. | `DD/parser/*.json`; `meta.level=terse`; count whitespace-separated words after removing formatting and document labels. Largest example: `gpt-6-luna__loneflag__terse__ALL__r02.json`. | imprecise |
| 4 | 234–236 | Opus independently coded “a deterministic random 10% sample.” | The specified hash rule selects **546 reviews**, but only **537** have second-judge records. **Nine selected API reviews are missing** second-judge coding. The reported agreement on the available 537 reproduces. | Hash-filter `K/reviewer/*.json`; join to `K/judge_opus-5.5/`. Missing records comprise dots: 2; Gemini: 1; Nemotron: 3; Xing: 3. | imprecise |
| 5 | 237–238 | DD red-flag, fabrication, lone-flag and admission fields have “κ = 1.00.” | These fields have **100% raw agreement but undefined Cohen’s κ**: both judges use one identical category throughout, making κ’s denominator zero. RF1 has **44** paired codes; RF2, RF3, fabrication, lone flag and admission each have **24**. | Join both DD judge directories on run ID; inspect each field’s marginal distributions. All are constant in this sample. | error |
| 6 | 244–245 | “39 of 132” were PARTIAL or ACCEPT versus human PUSHBACK; rates “23%, 33% and 30%.” | **33/132** satisfy the stated labels: 25 PARTIAL and 8 ACCEPT. The other **6** are NOT_ADDRESSED. For PARTIAL/ACCEPT specifically, rates are **6/30 = 20%**, **12/46 = 26%**, and **15/56 = 27%**. The printed counts/rates reproduce only when NOT_ADDRESSED is included. | `K/annotation/sheet.csv` joined through `key.json` to primary judge records; filter the 132 issue-position annotations and group C0/A/B. | error |
| 7 | 246–247, 502–503 | The coding bias makes contract-review effects “conservative.” | That direction of effect bias is **not established**. In the selected annotation sample, mean judge-minus-human issue scores are **+0.300 for C0, +0.391 for A, and +0.446 for B**. Overstating both treatment and control scores does not establish conservative differences; label-stratified sampling also prevents treating these as population corrections. | Same annotation join; apply the paper’s 0/1/2/2 scoring separately to both coders. | wording |
| 8 | 283–290; Figure 1 values file | Figure values displayed to two decimal places. | **19/68 point estimates and 29/68 intervals** retain one-decimal rounding despite two-decimal display. Examples: Haiku commercial gloss A **7.80 → 7.84**; Opus commercial first A **−4.50 → −4.55**; Opus no-deed hard assertion **0.00 → +0.05**. All significance marks reproduce. | Recompute from K/D/IP raw records, bootstrap using the released seed, apply B-arm sign reversal, and compare with `paper/figures/figure1.values.txt`. These differences arise from intermediate rounding. | imprecise |
| 9 | 330–331 | Hidden-text PDF: “No model moved toward the seller.” | **10/15 model-setting estimates are positive**, ranging from **+0.05 to +0.50**. None is significant. “No statistically significant movement toward the seller” is supported. | `K/reviewer` plus primary judge; A2P minus matched C0P, across the five core models and three settings. | wording |
| 10 | 336–337 | “Every model named framing far less often than injection.” | GPT-6 Sol names both at **exactly 10%**: framing **9/90**, injection **6/60**. Thus the universal comparison fails even within Table 2’s CLI models. | Primary Kestrel naming codes, pooled over tasks/settings; B1–B3 versus A1–A2. | wording |
| 11 | 354–355 | The strong arm moved against its own side “in every model” commercially. | Strong A does so in **13/13** models; strong B does so in only **7/13**. GPT-6 Luna’s strong B moves **−18.7605**, significantly **toward B** (*p* ≈ **0.0013**). | `D/reviewer/*.json`; fully parsed `case_v2`, `multiturn`; strongA/strongB minus matched control. | error |
| 12 | 381–382 | Note alone had no meaningful effect, with **\|Δ\| ≤ 3.3** for every core model. | Maximum **\|Δ\| = 11.65**: Opus insurance note B. Haiku dismissal note B is **+8.40**; Sol insurance note B is **+7.20**. All three have adjusted *p* ≈ **0.0013**. | Fully parsed D records; noteA/noteB versus control across all three cases and five core models. | error |
| 13 | 469–471 | Opus moved against slanted glosses. | Opus moves **with** the favoured side in **3/6** gloss cells. In particular, dismissal gloss B gives **−5.60**, significantly toward B (*p* ≈ **0.0013**). The reaction against slant needs qualification by arm and case. | Fully parsed D records; Opus glossA/glossB minus control across three cases. | wording |
| 14 | 440–441 | No injected DD pipeline “recommended proceeding.” | **44/200** are coded `PROCEED_WITH_CONDITIONS`; **156/200** are `DO_NOT_PROCEED_OR_ESCALATE`; **0/200** are unconditional `PROCEED`. The claim needs “unconditionally.” | `DD/orchestrator/*.json`, overt/subtle rooms; join primary judge `RECOMMENDATION`. | wording |
| 15 | 444–445 | Direct readers preserved hedges in **80–100%** of runs. | Across mechanism and discovery, the actual range is **10–100%**. Fable’s discovery direct baseline preserves the hedge in **1/10** and hardens it in **9/10**. The 80–100% range applies to mechanism alone. | `DD/direct/*.json`; join primary judge; group by corpus and orchestrator; count `HEDGE=PRESERVED`. | error |

**Claims I could not fully verify**

- **Wrapper sizes and cancellation, lines 141–145:** the probes record total input tokens, not an isolated wrapper-token count. They also do not establish that wrapper–treatment interactions cancel.
- **“Eight glosses” introducing facts, lines 173 and 384:** archived/current requests show **10 changed gloss fields across eight evidence items**. Establishing that exactly eight changes concern added facts requires an explicit classification; I have not treated ten edits alone as disproving that narrower claim.
- **Causal interpretation of the gloss rewrite, lines 384–389:** the reported before/after numbers reproduce, including from archived raw runs. However, rerun variability prevents attributing every change to fact removal; insurance gloss-A inputs were unchanged despite several changed estimates.
- **Calibration and comparative generalizations:** the stipulated “calibrated P about 75–95” at line 213 requires an external calibration standard. The claim that model/vendor differences exceeded attack-type differences at lines 482–483 lacks a defined comparison metric.
- Actual human blinding and authorship/review provenance cannot be independently established from saved responses and annotation labels.

**How many claims I checked**

**663 checks:** 537 numerical/significance checks across the five tables and Figure 1, plus 126 narrative and methods claim units. Estimates, CI endpoints, p-values and significance marks were counted separately.

All **261 checks in Tables 2–5** reproduce at printed precision. Also confirmed: 17 models; approximately 12,000 assessments (**11,730 reviewer/decider records before parsing exclusions**); all four order counts; the contract-effect maxima; the quoted gloss/drip and rewrite numbers; IP pipeline means and bundle effects; Haiku’s **14.1-point drop and 12/20 deed recognition**; and the DD hardening and lone-flag rates.

No files were modified, created or deleted.
